  "rules": {
    "mistel_offer_and_claim_scope_v1": "The frozen Mistel X-VIII page shows SKU B08T64B22G, a 122.99-dollar price, an 88-percent aggregate score across 12 reviews, and Cherry MX Silent Red among the selectable colors or switch variants. It describes a full-size 108-key USB-C board with PBT double-shot keycaps, programmable macro or remapping support, onboard memory, Windows and Mac compatibility, and non-RGB lighting. The page labels Cherry MX Silent Red as linear and silent and claims patented noise-reduction technology, shorter travel than Cherry MX Red, and suitability for offices. Those are seller-page statements about the listed configuration; the page supplies no sound-pressure value, membrane comparison, microphone result, through-wall result, or independent typing-feel test.",
    "silent_switch_dampener_experience_v1": "One author reports that Outemu Silent Cream Yellow tactile switches have rubber dampeners, feel crisp and silent to that author, and became a work daily driver because the author wanted an unobtrusive keyboard. The author worried that the dampeners might feel squishy but did not personally notice that effect, and reports that removing the dampeners removed the squishy feel at the expense of a deeper and louder sound. This is direct evidence that a dampened silent-switch design can trade sound character against feel for one person. It is not evidence about the linear Cherry MX Silent Red switch, the Mistel case, a membrane keyboard, decibels, a call microphone, or wall transmission.",
    "damping_path_and_mod_risk_v1": "The acoustic-quieting reference frames noise control as a source-to-observer chain involving source generation, sympathetic vibration, resonance, transmission through air or solids, and reflection. One separate hobbyist describes the trampoline mod, in which a 2 mm silicone ball under each switch shortens and quiets the downward stroke but makes bottoming out extremely squishy. The author's leather insert was only slightly quieter, shortened travel, could be difficult or impossible to remove, and could cause missed or duplicated keypresses when too thick. This supports a narrow downstroke-damping tradeoff and a one-switch safety test. It is not an O-ring result and cannot establish the behavior of O-rings, other switch events, the full keyboard enclosure, the desk, the room, a microphone, or a partition wall.",
    "matched_acoustic_measurement_boundary_v1": "A sound level meter uses a microphone to convert sound-pressure changes into an electrical signal and reports sound-pressure level in decibels; the reference also notes that results depend on frequency and time weighting and that meter readings do not map perfectly to perceived loudness. Sound Transmission Class rates how well a building partition attenuates airborne sound, and the reference describes a standardized frequency-dependent laboratory measurement rather than a keyboard label. The frozen keyboard pages contain none of the matched measurements needed to show that the Mistel, an O-ring modification, or the trampoline modification equals a membrane keyboard at the call microphone or through this apartment wall. The correct conclusion is unavailable comparison, not equality or failure.",
    "conditional_real_room_trial_v1": "If the buyer can verify the exact Cherry MX Silent Red variant and a usable return window, first compare a small switch sample or the returnable Mistel against the current nonmechanical or membrane-style reference in the actual setup. Keep desk mat, typing passage, typing force, microphone position and gain, call processing, door and wall locations, and time of day fixed. Record both bottom-out and release noise, stabilized keys, ordinary call audio, and a same-location next-room or wall-side observation. Type for a sustained session and check shorter travel, fatigue, squish, missed presses, duplicate presses, and layout fit. Predeclare a keep threshold based on the actual call and neighbor constraint, not the seller's word silent. Do not perform a hard-to-remove whole-board under-stem modification before a one-switch test, and do not treat the present material as proof that O-rings solve the problem.",
    "evidence_bounded_quiet_keyboard_decision_v1": "The admissible decision is a conditional, returnable trial of the exact Mistel Silent Red variant only when the buyer can make a matched real-room comparison and accepts the residual uncertainty. The listing identifies an available silent-switch board but does not prove its acoustics or feel. The Outemu experience establishes only that one different dampened tactile switch can be unobtrusive without noticeable squish for one author. The trampoline experience establishes only that under-stem damping can trade shorter and quieter downstrokes against squish, irreversibility, and activation errors, and it is not O-ring evidence. If membrane-equivalent call or neighbor quietness is a hard requirement and cannot be verified before the return deadline, keep or choose a known nonmechanical option or defer; do not buy or modify on an equivalence claim that the corpus does not establish."
  },
